Unemployment Analysis is a data analysis project that focuses on studying unemployment rates in different regions of India and understanding
how unemployment changed over time. The main objective of this task is to use data analysis and visualization techniques to identify regional 
and temporal trends, especially the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on unemployment rates. 
In this task, we use Python, pandas, matplotlib, seaborn, and Jupyter Notebook to work with the unemployment dataset. First, the dataset is loaded 
and examined to understand its structure. We check the number of rows and columns, missing values, and data types, and convert the required data
into suitable formats for analysis. 
After preparing the data, Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is performed. We calculate the average unemployment rate for different regions and 
analyse how unemployment rates change from month to month. These analyses help us understand which regions have higher or lower unemployment 
and how unemployment varies with time. 
We also create a time-series line chart to observe unemployment rates over time for at least three major states or regions. A bar chart is used 
to identify the top 10 states with the highest average unemployment rates. These visualizations make it easier to compare unemployment levels 
between different states and identify important patterns. 
Another important part of the project is the correlation heatmap. It is used to study the relationship between the unemployment rate, 
employment rate, and labour participation rate. This helps us understand how these different employment-related factors are related to one another.
A major focus of this task is understanding the effect of COVID-19 on unemployment in India. By analysing unemployment data over time,
we can examine changes during the pandemic period and compare trends across different regions.
